dict(id="b1-shelter", level="B1", title="The Volunteer", tr="Gönüllü", text="""
After she retired from her job as an accountant, Gül felt lost. For forty years, her days had been full of meetings and deadlines. Suddenly she had nothing to do, and the weeks seemed very long.

Her daughter suggested volunteering, so Gül contacted an animal shelter on the edge of the city. The shelter {looked after} abandoned dogs and cats, and it depended completely on volunteers and donations.

On her first day, Gül was shocked. The building was old, the kennels were crowded, and the noise was incredible. But the manager, a young vet named Ozan, explained that every animal there had a story. Some had been found on the street; others had been {left behind} when their owners {moved house}.

Gül started going three mornings a week. She fed the animals, cleaned the kennels and took the dogs for walks. Soon she used her old skills too: she organised the accounts of the shelter and helped write an application for funding from the local council. The application was successful, and the shelter was able to build new kennels.

Her favourite was an old, half-blind dog called Pamuk, whom nobody wanted to adopt. {In the end}, Gül took him home herself.

"People think volunteering is about giving," she says. "But I have received much more than I have given. It gave me a reason to {get up} in the morning."
"""),
dict(id="b1-remote", level="B1", title="Working from Home", tr="Evden Çalışmak", text="""
When Kerem's company told its staff they could work from home permanently, he was delighted. There would be no more crowded trains, no more expensive coffee and no more sitting in an open-plan office next to a colleague who talked on the phone all day.

For the first few months, it was perfect. He saved two hours a day on travel, and he used the extra time to {go running} and cook proper meals. He could also concentrate better, and his manager was pleased with his work.

However, after a while, he began to notice some problems. Because his desk was in his bedroom, he found it hard to {switch off} in the evenings. He often answered emails at eleven o'clock at night. Some days, he realised he hadn't left the flat or spoken to anyone {face to face}.

He also missed the small conversations in the office: the jokes at the coffee machine and the chance to ask a quick question. New colleagues joined the team, and he had never met them {in person}.

{In the end}, Kerem found a compromise. He now works from home three days a week and goes to the office on the other two. He also bought a small desk for the {living room}, and he closes his laptop at six o'clock every evening.

"Working from home is great," he says, "but you need to set limits. Otherwise, your home becomes your office."
"""),
dict(id="b1-bookshop", level="B1", title="The Old Bookshop", tr="Eski Kitapçı", text="""
On a narrow street in the old part of the city, there is a bookshop that has been open for more than eighty years. It is owned by Cemal, who inherited it from his grandfather. The shelves reach the ceiling, and there are piles of books on the floor, on the stairs and even in the window.

A few years ago, the bookshop almost closed. Fewer people were buying books, and those who did usually ordered them online because it was cheaper and faster. The rent kept rising, and some months Cemal couldn't pay his bills.

Then his granddaughter, Elif, who was studying marketing at university, had an idea. She opened {social media} accounts for the shop and posted short videos: Cemal recommending a book, Cemal finding a rare {first edition}, Cemal telling stories about famous writers who had visited the shop.

The videos became surprisingly popular. Young people started coming to the shop, not just to buy books but to meet the old man from the videos. Elif also organised poetry evenings and a reading club for children on Saturday mornings.

Today the shop is busier than it has been for decades. Cemal still doesn't use a computer, and he still writes every sale in a notebook. "The internet nearly killed my shop," he says with a laugh, "and then it saved it."
"""),
dict(id="b1-gapyear", level="B1", title="A Gap Year", tr="Ara Yıl", text="""
After finishing {high school}, Lara decided not to go straight to university. Her parents were worried. They thought she would lose interest in studying and waste a year. But Lara had a plan: she wanted to take a {gap year}, work, travel and decide what she really wanted to study.

For the first six months, she worked in a supermarket and saved almost all of her salary. It was boring, but it taught her the value of money and hard work.

Then she spent four months travelling in South America. She stayed in cheap hostels, learned basic Spanish and volunteered for a month on a project that built schools in a small village in Peru. There, she worked with engineers and saw how a simple building could change a community.

The experience changed her plans completely. Before her {gap year}, she had wanted to study business because it seemed a safe choice. When she came back, she applied to study {civil engineering} instead.

Now in her final year at university, Lara says the {gap year} was the best decision she ever made. "I came back more independent and more confident," she says. "And for the first time, I knew why I was studying."
"""),
dict(id="b1-marathon", level="B1", title="The Marathon", tr="Maraton", text="""
Two years ago, Tarık could not run for more than five minutes without stopping. He was overweight, he smoked, and he spent most evenings on the sofa. Then, on his fortieth birthday, his doctor gave him a warning: if he didn't change his lifestyle, he would have serious health problems.

Tarık decided to make a change. He {gave up} smoking and started walking every day. After a month, he began to run very short distances, using a free app that told him when to run and when to walk. It was hard, and some days he wanted to quit, but he {kept going}.

Six months later, he ran his first five-kilometre race. He finished near the back, but he felt fantastic. He joined a running club, where he met people of all ages and abilities. They encouraged him to set a bigger goal: a marathon.

The training took a whole year. He ran four times a week, in rain, wind and even snow. He also changed his diet, eating more vegetables and less sugar.

Last April, Tarık ran the city marathon. The last ten kilometres were the hardest thing he had ever done, and his legs were screaming. But he crossed the {finish line} in four hours and thirty-two minutes. His wife and children were waiting for him, holding a sign that said "Our hero".

"I am not fast," he says. "But two years ago I couldn't run to the {bus stop}. If I can do this, anyone can."
"""),
