dict(id="b2-fashion", level="B2", title="Fast Fashion", tr="Hızlı Moda", text="""
Clothes have never been cheaper. A T-shirt can cost less than a cup of coffee, and some retailers release new collections every week. This business model, {known as} {fast fashion}, has made fashionable clothing available to almost everyone. Yet its hidden costs are becoming increasingly difficult to ignore.

The environmental impact is considerable. The textile industry is one of the largest consumers of water in the world, and producing a single pair of jeans can require thousands of litres. Synthetic fabrics such as polyester are made from oil and release tiny plastic fibres when they are washed, many of which {end up} in rivers and oceans. Meanwhile, millions of tonnes of clothing are {thrown away} each year, often after being worn only {a handful of} times.

There are also serious concerns about {working conditions}. Much of the clothing in our shops is produced in countries where wages are low and safety regulations are poorly enforced. Garment workers, the majority of whom are women, frequently work long hours for very little pay.

In response, a growing number of consumers are changing their habits. {Second-hand} shopping, once associated with poverty, has become fashionable, particularly among young people. Clothing rental services and repair workshops are also gaining popularity, and some brands now offer to {take back} old garments for recycling.

Critics, however, argue that individual choices will not be enough. They {call for} stricter regulation, including requirements for companies to disclose where and how their products are made. Others {point out} that cheap clothing benefits low-income families, who may not be able to afford more sustainable alternatives.

What seems clear is that the true price of a cheap T-shirt is not the one on the label. Someone, somewhere, is paying the difference.
"""),
dict(id="b2-sleep", level="B2", title="Sleep and the Teenage Brain", tr="Uyku ve Ergen Beyni", text="""
Every school morning, millions of parents face the same battle: persuading a sleepy teenager to {get out of bed}. It is tempting to blame laziness or late-night phone use, and these certainly {play a part}. However, research suggests that something more fundamental is {going on}.

During adolescence, the internal clock of the body shifts. The hormone melatonin, which makes us feel sleepy, is released later in the evening than in children or adults. {As a result}, many teenagers genuinely find it difficult to {fall asleep} before eleven o'clock, even if they go to bed earlier. Since adolescents need roughly eight to ten hours of sleep, an early school start leaves many of them chronically {sleep-deprived}.

The consequences extend well beyond tiredness. Lack of sleep has been linked to poorer concentration, lower exam results, mood problems and an increased risk of accidents among young drivers. Some studies have also found connections with anxiety and depression.

In response, a number of schools have experimented with later start times. The results have generally been encouraging: students sleep longer, attend more regularly and, in some cases, achieve better grades. Teachers report that students in early lessons are noticeably more alert.

Nevertheless, the change is not straightforward. Later start times can create problems for parents who need to get to work, for school transport and for afternoon activities and sports. Some critics also worry that teenagers will simply {stay up} even later.

Experts suggest that school timetables alone cannot solve the problem. Limiting screens before bed, keeping regular sleep patterns at weekends and understanding that the tiredness of teenagers is partly biological may all help. In other words, we are often asking teenagers to perform at the time of day when their brains are least prepared for it.
"""),
dict(id="b2-tourism", level="B2", title="Too Many Tourists", tr="Fazla Turist", text="""
For centuries, the small island of Mirela was known mainly to fishermen and {a handful of} adventurous travellers. Then, about fifteen years ago, a series of photographs of its turquoise bays {went viral} on {social media}. Within a decade, the number of annual visitors had risen from a few thousand to well over a million.

At first, the boom was welcomed. Tourism brought jobs, new restaurants and investment in roads and the airport. Young people who had previously left the island to find work began to stay. Property owners became wealthy almost overnight by renting their homes to visitors.

But the benefits {came at a price}. In summer, the narrow streets of the main town became so crowded that residents struggled to get to work. Water shortages became common, as hotels and swimming pools consumed far more than the limited supply of the island. Rents soared, and many local families, unable to compete with {short-term} holiday rentals, were forced to move to the mainland. Traditional shops selling bread and household goods were gradually replaced by souvenir stalls and cocktail bars.

The local government has now introduced a series of measures to manage the problem. {Cruise ships} are limited to two per day, a tourist tax funds the maintenance of beaches and paths, and new permits for holiday rentals have been frozen. Visitors are also encouraged to come outside the {peak season}.

Opinion on the island remains divided. Business owners fear that restrictions will drive tourists elsewhere, while groups of residents argue that the measures do not {go far enough}. What almost everyone agrees on is that the island cannot simply return to how it was. The challenge now is to find a form of tourism that the island and its people can survive.
"""),
dict(id="b2-ageing", level="B2", title="The Ageing Society", tr="Yaşlanan Toplum", text="""
Across much of the world, people are living longer than ever before, while fewer babies are being born. In some countries, more than a quarter of the population is already over sixty-five, and the proportion is rising. This demographic shift is often described as a crisis, but it may be more accurate to see it as both a challenge and an opportunity.

The challenges are real. As the {working-age} population shrinks, there are fewer taxpayers to fund pensions and healthcare for a growing number of retired people. Health systems face increasing demand for the treatment of chronic conditions such as diabetes and dementia, and many countries already report shortages of carers. Some governments have responded by raising the {retirement age}, a policy that is understandably unpopular.

Yet the picture is not entirely negative. Older people today are, on average, healthier and more active than previous generations. Many continue working, either {full-time} or {part-time}, and they contribute enormously as volunteers, as carers for grandchildren and as consumers. Businesses have begun to recognise the economic potential of this {so-called} silver economy, developing products and services designed specifically for older customers.

Technology may also help. Sensors that detect falls, video consultations with doctors and robots that help with lifting are already being tested in {care homes}. Critics warn, however, that technology cannot replace human contact, and that loneliness is one of the most serious health risks for elderly people living alone.

Ultimately, adapting to an ageing society will require changes in attitudes {as well as} policies. Designing cities with more benches and accessible transport, encouraging flexible retirement and valuing the experience of older workers could allow longer lives to be not only healthier but also more fulfilling.
"""),
dict(id="b2-ai", level="B2", title="AI in the Classroom", tr="Sınıfta Yapay Zekâ", text="""
When powerful AI writing tools became freely available, many teachers reacted with alarm. Students could now produce a competent essay in seconds, and traditional homework suddenly seemed pointless. Some schools responded by banning the tools altogether. Others, however, have chosen a different path.

At one {secondary school}, the English department decided that, {rather than} pretending the technology did not exist, they would teach students to use it critically. In one assignment, students ask an AI tool to write an argumentative essay, then work in groups to evaluate it. They check its facts, identify weaknesses in its reasoning and rewrite it to make the argument more convincing. Teachers report that students often discover that the essays of the machine are fluent but shallow, and that they sometimes contain confident statements that are simply wrong.

Supporters of this approach argue that it reflects the reality of the modern workplace, where such tools are increasingly common. Knowing how to question and improve machine-generated content, they suggest, may become as important as writing {from scratch}.

Nevertheless, many educators remain cautious. Writing is not only a way of communicating but also a way of thinking; struggling to organise ideas on a blank page is part of how understanding develops. If students {rely on} AI from the start, they may never develop that ability. There are also concerns about fairness, since students with access to more advanced tools may gain an advantage, and about the privacy of the data that students enter into these systems.

{As a result}, many schools are now redesigning assessment {rather than} relying on detection software, which has proved unreliable. More work is completed in class, oral presentations are becoming more common, and students are increasingly asked to explain the process behind their work, not just submit the final product.

The debate is {far from} settled. What is clear is that the question is no longer whether AI belongs in education, but how it can be used without undermining the very skills that education is meant to develop.
"""),
